# [M5.0] D27 axis 3 -- invalid UTF-8 (extract Sec 2.6(a)-(e)/2.6.1). 27 blocks
# (9 measured ill-formed kinds x 3 positions: before/after/through the bad
# bytes), 4 subjects each -> 108 cases.
#
# PROMOTED (lane utfprom, [M5.0] stage 2 close): all 27 blocks compile and
# match under `-e utf8` now, zero divergences from pcrec's own live
# behaviour across all 108 cases -- the ill-formed-subject barrier ruling
# below holds exactly as measured.
#
# RULING UNDER TEST (2.6): an ill-formed byte sequence matches NOTHING under
# pcrec's `utf8` encoding -- no validation pass, no error return -- which is
# PCRE2_MATCH_INVALID_UTF's barrier answer "for free from the automaton's
# structure". So the oracle for every case below is libpcre2 under
# PCRE2_MATCH_INVALID_UTF, never plain PCRE2_UTF (which would ERRM-refuse the
# whole subject and is NOT what pcrec promises).
# 9 measured kinds (this cell's libpcre2 10.37 2021-05-26, error code under PLAIN PCRE2_UTF -- 2.6(b)'s whole-subject precondition, NOT the MATCH_INVALID_UTF barrier the corpus's own oracle uses below): isolated-continuation=-22, illegal-fe-ff=-23, trunc-missing-1=-3, trunc-missing-2=-4, overlong-2byte=-17, overlong-3byte=-18, surrogate=-16, above-10ffff=-15, five-byte-form=-13

# axis3 block isolated-continuation-before | kind=isolated-continuation bad=80 | pattern: a
#   [canonical] m "a\x80" 0 1   # oracle=PCRE2_MATCH_INVALID_UTF
#   [with-trailing-valid] m "a\x80z" 0 1   # oracle=PCRE2_MATCH_INVALID_UTF
#   [non-member] n "b\x80"   # oracle=PCRE2_MATCH_INVALID_UTF
#   [bad-alone-no-a] n "\x80"   # oracle=PCRE2_MATCH_INVALID_UTF
# pcre2-only
pattern a
encoding utf8
m "a\x80" 0 1
m "a\x80z" 0 1
n "b\x80"
n "\x80"

